---
/**
 * docs/mcp.astro — how to connect an MCP client to Apelles (D-255's content,
 * D-264's presentation and brand).
 *
 * What it is: the one page on this site written for a developer rather than a
 * visitor. The friendliness and restraint the marketing pages hold themselves
 * to do not apply here — an integrator needs exact commands, exact paths and
 * exact ports, and softening those would make the page worse.
 * What it does NOT do: invent an API shape. Every command, path, port, tool
 * name and error fragment is transcribed from real repo sources — mcp/server.py,
 * mcp/README.md, the control server's documented contract, and
 * docs/notes/mcp-tool-coverage.md — and tests/mcp-data.test.ts re-checks the
 * numbers, the tool names, the port variable and the error strings on every run.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { TOOL_GROUPS, TOOL_TOTALS, GAPS, CONNECTION, VERIFIED_ON } from '../../data/mcp.ts';

const sections = [
  { id: 'how', label: 'How it works' },
  { id: 'install', label: 'Install' },
  { id: 'connect', label: 'Connect a client' },
  { id: 'first', label: 'First calls' },
  { id: 'tools', label: 'The tools' },
  { id: 'http', label: 'The HTTP surface' },
  { id: 'trouble', label: 'Troubleshooting' },
];
---

<Base
  title="Connect an agent to Apelles — MCP setup"
  description="Real setup instructions for driving Apelles from Claude Code or any MCP client: install the stdio server, connect it, and call the 106 shipped tools across the editor and the colorist."
>
  <article class="doc">
    <header class="shell hd">
      <p class="eyebrow">Documentation</p>
      <h1>Connect an agent</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Apelles ships a stdio MCP server. Point any MCP client at it and that client can drive the
        running desktop app &mdash; {TOOL_TOTALS.shipped} tools across the editor and the colorist,
        every one of them going through the same store action the GUI button calls.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="shell layout">
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <p class="eyebrow">On this page</p>
        {sections.map((s) => <a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a>)}
      </nav>

      <div class="prose">
        <section id="how">
          <h2>How it works</h2>
          <p>
            The server itself holds no grading or editing logic. Each tool posts one op to a small
            HTTP control server running inside the app, which forwards it to the frontend, which
            applies it through the identical store action a button press would. That is why an
            agent&rsquo;s edit moves real sliders, lands on the same undo stack, and why reading
            state back reflects whatever you changed by hand a moment ago.
          </p>
          <pre class="diagram"><code>MCP client
  │  stdio
{CONNECTION.serverPath}
  │  HTTP  POST /op {`{op, args}`}
{CONNECTION.controlServerPath}   (control server, in the app, port {CONNECTION.defaultPort})
  │  Tauri events
the frontend  (owns the state)
  │  the SAME store actions the GUI buttons call
render  →  {`{ rendered frame, histogram, adjustments }`}</code></pre>
          <p class="note">
            Mutating Colorist tools return the re-rendered frame, its histogram and a compact scope
            summary &mdash; the new measurement, for free, after the change.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="install">
          <h2>Install</h2>
          <p>From a clone of the repository:</p>
          <pre><code>cd mcp
python3 -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt</code></pre>
          <p>
            You also need the Apelles desktop app running. The control server binds when the app
            starts, and the bridge is live while a project is open.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="connect">
          <h2>Connect a client</h2>
          <h3>Claude Code</h3>
          <pre><code>claude mcp add apelles -- \
  /ABS/PATH/TO/REPO/mcp/.venv/bin/python \
  /ABS/PATH/TO/REPO/mcp/server.py</code></pre>

          <h3>Any client that takes a JSON config</h3>
          <p>
            It is an ordinary stdio MCP server, so the standard client config shape works unchanged
            &mdash; the command is the virtualenv&rsquo;s Python, the argument is the server file:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "apelles": {
      "command": "/ABS/PATH/TO/REPO/mcp/.venv/bin/python",
      "args": ["/ABS/PATH/TO/REPO/mcp/server.py"]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>

          <h3>A non-default port</h3>
          <p>
            The control server listens on <code>{CONNECTION.defaultPort}</code> unless you override
            it. If you changed it on the app side, set the same value in the server&rsquo;s
            environment:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{CONNECTION.portEnvVar}=19999</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="first">
          <h2>First calls</h2>
          <p>Two calls are worth making before anything else, and both are cheap:</p>
          <dl class="defs">
            <dt><code>get_state</code></dt>
            <dd>
              What is loaded, the current primary adjustments, a mask summary, the multi-shot
              session and the open project. It reflects manual edits, so it is also how you check
              what the human just did.
            </dd>
            <dt><code>editor_get_capabilities</code></dt>
            <dd>
              Static reference data with no round trip to the app &mdash; the non-obvious
              compositing rules, the per-property rule that a keyframe silently beats a transform
              written for the same property, and the known rough edges. Read it once per session
              before your first transform, keyframe or export.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section id="tools">
          <h2>The tools</h2>
          <p>
            Counted directly from <code>{CONNECTION.serverPath}</code> on {VERIFIED_ON}:
            {' '}{TOOL_TOTALS.all} tool definitions exist, of which {TOOL_TOTALS.debugOnly} are
            development-only and compiled out of a production build, leaving
            {' '}{TOOL_TOTALS.shipped} that ship.
          </p>

          {
            TOOL_GROUPS.map((g) => (
              <div class="grp">
                <h3>
                  {g.name} <span class="cnt mono">{g.count}</span>
                </h3>
                <p>{g.blurb}</p>
                <ul class="names mono">
                  {g.sample.map((n) => (
                    <li>{n}</li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              </div>
            ))
          }

          <div class="grp">
            <h3>What is still missing</h3>
            <p>
              Two real gaps remain, listed here rather than left for you to discover against a
              live app:
            </p>
            <ul class="gaps">
              {GAPS.map((g) => <li>{g}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="http">
          <h2>The HTTP surface</h2>
          <p>
            You do not need this to use the MCP server, but it is what the server talks to and it
            is worth knowing it exists. The control server accepts one endpoint:
          </p>
          <pre><code>POST http://127.0.0.1:{CONNECTION.defaultPort}/op
Content-Type: application/json

{`{ "op": "get_state", "args": {} }`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            It is bound to loopback only. A request the app cannot answer within its bridge timeout
            comes back as a <code>504</code>, which the MCP server turns into a readable error
            rather than a hang &mdash; long-running work (a depth track, a transcript, a video
            analysis) is always start-then-poll for exactly this reason.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="trouble">
          <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
          <dl class="defs">
            <dt>&ldquo;Cannot reach the &hellip; control server&rdquo;</dt>
            <dd>
              The app is not running, or it is on another port. Start it, or set
              <code> {CONNECTION.portEnvVar}</code> to match.
            </dd>
            <dt>&ldquo;&hellip; did not respond (its window may be closed or busy)&rdquo;</dt>
            <dd>
              A 504 from the bridge: the window is closed, or nothing is open. The bridge only runs
              while the editor view is mounted.
            </dd>
            <dt>A response with a histogram but no image</dt>
            <dd>
              A known limitation, not a failure. The app renders to a native GPU surface, so the
              returned frame is a best-effort headless re-render. The histogram, scopes and
              adjustments are always accurate.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .hd {
    /* Minimum clears the fixed island nav (D-283) at any viewport width. */
    padding-top: clamp(4.5rem, 3.5rem + 4vw, 6.5rem);
    padding-bottom: clamp(2rem, 1.5rem + 2vw, 3rem);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--edge);
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-4);
    margin: 1.25rem 0 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 300;
  }

  .layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 200px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2rem, 1rem + 4vw, 5rem);
    padding-top: 3rem;
    padding-bottom: 5rem;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .toc {
    position: sticky;
    top: 86px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.55rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .toc .eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }
  .toc a {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--pigment-basalt-soft);
    transition: color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out);
  }
  .toc a:hover {
    color: var(--pigment-terracotta);
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: 72ch;
  }

  .prose section + section {
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }

  .prose h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    padding-bottom: 0.75rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--edge);
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .prose h3 {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    margin: 2rem 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .prose p {
    color: var(--pigment-basalt-soft);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  /* A listing, set the way a listing is set in print: dark, tight, and clearly
     a different kind of object from the prose around it. */
  .prose pre {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem;
    background: var(--pigment-basalt);
    color: var(--pigment-marble);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .diagram {
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pigment-marble) 78%, transparent) !important;
  }

  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.88em;
  }

  .prose p > code,
  .defs dt code {
    color: var(--pigment-terracotta);
  }

  .note {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--pigment-ochre);
    padding-left: 0.9rem;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .defs {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .defs dt {
    color: var(--pigment-basalt);
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    padding-top: 0.9rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--edge);
  }
  .defs dd {
    margin: 0.45rem 0 1rem;
    color: var(--pigment-basalt-soft);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .grp {
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--edge);
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .grp h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    font-size: var(--step-2);
  }
  .cnt {
    color: var(--pigment-terracotta);
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .gaps {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.9rem;
  }
  .gaps li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--pigment-basalt-soft);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .gaps li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.62em;
    width: 5px;
    height: 5px;
    border-radius: 1px;
    background: var(--pigment-ochre);
  }

  .names {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.3rem;
  }
  .names li {
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    color: var(--pigment-basalt-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--edge-strong);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.45rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 880px) {
    .layout {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
    .toc {
      position: static;
      flex-direction: row;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 0.75rem 1.25rem;
    }
    .toc .eyebrow {
      width: 100%;
    }
  }
</style>
